          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Galen</strong><br>\nGalen was born in Pergamon, a Greek city that is now in modern-day Turkey, around the year 129 AD. His full name was Aelius Galenus, and he was raised in a wealthy family which allowed him to receive a good education. At 16, he started studying medicine, a field he was deeply interested in due to his understanding of the importance of health and the human body's complexity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Galen's Medical Training</strong><br>\nGalen received his medical education in the famous Asclepieion of Pergamon, a sanctuary dedicated to the Greek god of medicine, Asclepius. He traveled extensively to further his knowledge, studying at Smyrna, Corinth, and Alexandria, which were all renowned centers of learning at the time. These travels allowed him to be exposed to various medical theories and practices.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Medicine</strong><br>\nGalen's medical theories dominated Western medicine for over a millennium. He performed extensive anatomical dissections, albeit mostly on animals, and his findings on the circulatory and nervous systems were significant. Although some of his conclusions were incorrect, they laid the foundation for future medical discoveries.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
