            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Galen's Philosophical Views</strong><br>\nAs a philosopher, Galen was influenced by the teachings of Plato and Aristotle, integrating their ideas into his medical practices. He believed that a physician should have a broad education, which includes philosophy, as it enhances the understanding of the body in relation to the soul.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Galen's Writing and Research</strong><br>\nGalen was a prolific writer, authoring about 500 works on various subjects, although less than a third survive to this day. His writings encompassed philosophy, medicine, logic, and ethics, and were considered authoritative texts in medical schools for centuries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Practice and Personal Experience</strong><br>\nGalen's practical experience was vast. He served as a surgeon to gladiators in Pergamon, which provided him with firsthand experience in treating severe injuries and understanding human anatomy. His success in this role earned him a high reputation as a physician.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
