          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence on Later Medicine</strong><br>\nGalen's work greatly influenced Islamic medicine and was adopted by physicians in the Islamic Golden Age. They translated and expanded upon his works, which later filtered back into Europe through Arabic to Latin translations. Thus, Galen's work bridged ancient and medieval medicine.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Opposition and Criticism</strong><br>\nDespite his contributions, Galen's work was not without its critics. Over time, some of his anatomical and physiological conclusions were challenged, particularly during the Renaissance when more accurate human dissections became the norm and disproved many of his animal-based findings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Memory</strong><br>\nGalen's legacy is monumental in the history of medicine. While many of his specific findings have been revised or refuted, his approach to medicine as a science that combines theory with practice has endured. He is remembered as one of the greatest physicians of the Roman Empire and a significant figure in medical history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
